WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.294 --> 00:00:24.294
[MUSIC]

00:00:42.264 --> 00:00:46.280
Welcome to the Leadership Society,
Reimagining Work Post COVID.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.250
I'm Brian Lowry and today we're talking
about education, citizens and workers,

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.568
education and capitalist democracy.

00:00:53.568 --> 00:00:57.925
And I'm really excited to talk
to two distinguished guests,

00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:02.890
Rachel Carlson, Co-founder and
CEO of Guild Education.

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:07.499
Rachel is the co-founder and CEO and
their company is on a mission to unlock

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:12.910
opportunity for America's workforce
through education and upskilling.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.290
And I'm also talking to David Osborne.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:18.190
Dave is author or
co-author of six nonfiction books,

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.981
including Reinventing America's Schools,
Creating a 21st Century School System and

00:01:22.981 --> 00:01:26.650
Reinventing Government,
a New York Times bestseller in 1992.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:27.311
So thanks for coming.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.190
Great, so I'm just going to jump right in.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.071
[COUGH] As we think about
reimagining work post COVID,

00:01:36.071 --> 00:01:39.090
we have to think about education and
training, right?

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:43.720
So to have a healthy economy,
to have a well functioning society,

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.026
we have to train people to
be able to do jobs, and

00:01:47.026 --> 00:01:51.970
also to engage in democracy in
a compelling and competent way.

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:53.413
So the question I have for

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:57.690
you two is how do you think about
the function of education in our society?

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.720
Where do you place the emphasis?

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.588
So David, I'll start with you.
&gt;&gt; Well that's a subject of a lot

00:02:03.588 --> 00:02:08.900
of dispute in American society.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.665
People have different views about that,
the way I look at it,

00:02:13.665 --> 00:02:16.240
education has two big missions.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.421
One is to help everyone, Develop in
a way that they can live their own

00:02:23.421 --> 00:02:29.690
life to the fullest to realize
their fullest potential.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.255
And the other is to help
them develop the skills and

00:02:32.255 --> 00:02:35.670
knowledge to be successful
in a modern economy.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.741
I think it's those two things,

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:43.980
there are other things we
need from education as well.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:49.260
It would be really nice if
the schools were preparing

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.190
people to be productive
citizens in a democracy.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:03:00.395
They often fail on that score,
as we've seen in recent years,

00:03:00.395 --> 00:03:04.240
but I think those are the two
that I emphasize.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:07.413
&gt;&gt; Rachel, what about you?

00:03:07.413 --> 00:03:12.126
Yeah, you know my views on this have
changed quite a bit, I joke that I was

00:03:12.126 --> 00:03:16.994
sort of born on third base, I was
grateful to go to Stanford undergrad, but

00:03:16.994 --> 00:03:21.710
I'm a legacy and make no illusions
about the fact that I'm clear why I got

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.460
the leg up, I stood on shoulders, right?

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:29.524
And so I really thought it was more about,

00:03:29.524 --> 00:03:35.448
I'll call it the some of the broader
debates that happen tied

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:41.890
to the educated civic population and
self actualization.

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:45.076
I've actually been able to see a path
through a lot of that debate now,

00:03:45.076 --> 00:03:47.190
which is unmatched to
how David described it.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:51.490
I think if we view education as a bit
more like a Maslow's Hierarchy of needs.

00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.660
There's a ground floor that
we have to provide to people.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.022
And that ground floor has to start with
the ability to provide for my family or

00:03:59.022 --> 00:04:02.240
know that I one day will be
able to provide for my family.

00:04:03.375 --> 00:04:06.045
That's so rooted in the American ethos.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:09.925
And the next level is I want to do
better than my parents did, right?

00:04:09.925 --> 00:04:14.743
That's the second part of the American
ethos of how we think about the American

00:04:14.743 --> 00:04:17.809
dream and doing better,
and being more capable or

00:04:17.809 --> 00:04:23.020
ability to have more flexibility or
the middle class family sustaining wage.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:26.975
Then above that what happens is people who
after they solve for those two things,

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:30.349
which makes all the sense in the world
from a Maslow's perspective,

00:04:30.349 --> 00:04:34.325
will then they want to learn more to think
about how can they be an engaged citizen.

00:04:34.325 --> 00:04:36.881
How can they be a contributing
member of their community,

00:04:36.881 --> 00:04:40.550
you see people then return to and really
embrace a lifelong learning perspective.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.804
So, that's my worldview today.

00:04:44.804 --> 00:04:45.759
&gt;&gt; Got it.
[CROSSTALK]

00:04:45.759 --> 00:04:47.904
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that isn't a really compelling

00:04:47.904 --> 00:04:51.934
way to put it which makes me think of
something that I'm impressed that you

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:56.455
are willing to say hey, I was a legacy,
I don't have any illusions around that.

00:04:56.455 --> 00:04:59.815
I think that's often
an uncomfortable thing to say.

00:04:59.815 --> 00:05:03.794
And I wonder what it makes you
think about education as a way to

00:05:03.794 --> 00:05:06.300
maintain things as they are, right?

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:12.310
So we typically think about education as a
way to improve the functioning of society.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:14.560
I wonder as you given that comment,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.103
if you think about how it might
actually maintain things as they are.

00:05:19.103 --> 00:05:20.099
&gt;&gt; Man, yeah,

00:05:20.099 --> 00:05:25.630
okay we'll go deeper like because I
have a lot of feelings about this.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:30.335
I obviously I spend my day to day helping
America's frontline workers have access to

00:05:30.335 --> 00:05:31.430
education.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.506
So I obviously spend a lot of my time
trying to disrupt the barriers that I'd

00:05:35.506 --> 00:05:39.196
argue are heavily oriented around
maintenance, but I think part of

00:05:39.196 --> 00:05:43.623
the important thing to acknowledge though
is that nobody's trying to do wrong.

00:05:43.623 --> 00:05:47.910
I spent a couple years really deep
in the community college system.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:52.856
And what I saw there was a community
colleges have an average graduation

00:05:52.856 --> 00:05:57.720
rate of 8 to 20% depending on
the school and how you slice the data,

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.690
but I never met a bad person
working one of those schools.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:05.500
I met people who were deeply
dedicated to the mission in the work.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:09.473
The problem is we built those schools for
a mission in the 50s and

00:06:09.473 --> 00:06:13.158
60s which was sending our young
men to school after a war and

00:06:13.158 --> 00:06:17.732
creating purpose and meaning for
that crowd tied to an economic need then.

00:06:17.732 --> 00:06:22.448
So there's an example and I think we
don't talk enough about that one and

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:27.088
then I'll jump right into the hot bed
that the New York Times would write

00:06:27.088 --> 00:06:29.562
a story about everyday if they could,

00:06:29.562 --> 00:06:33.535
which is the preservation of
different types of privilege.

00:06:34.815 --> 00:06:38.911
What bothered me a lot in my year working
in admissions, and by the way, I'm

00:06:38.911 --> 00:06:43.460
proud Stanford alum, I should say that and
I think we need to keep changing, right?

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.960
In my year working in Stanford admissions,
it was frustrating to see that there were

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.073
some elements of diversity that the school
was able to push boundaries on, and

00:06:54.073 --> 00:06:59.107
others which had financial implications
including welcoming and more folks

00:06:59.107 --> 00:07:04.762
from the community college system or just
18 year olds from low income backgrounds,

00:07:04.762 --> 00:07:09.124
that was a harder choice to make
when you looked at aggregate data.

00:07:09.124 --> 00:07:11.738
And so,
I think there is a lot of maintenance,

00:07:11.738 --> 00:07:16.505
maintenance of an outdated higher red
system, maintenance of privilege.

00:07:16.505 --> 00:07:20.632
And we've got to change that if we
want to have a united citizenship back to

00:07:20.632 --> 00:07:21.241
the goal,

00:07:21.241 --> 00:07:25.654
if education wants to be productive
towards the ends of united citizenship.

00:07:25.654 --> 00:07:28.703
I think the root of our
populism right now is really,

00:07:28.703 --> 00:07:32.388
really connected to some of
the flaws of our education system.

00:07:32.388 --> 00:07:36.863
&gt;&gt; I liked that you referenced the
creation of the community college system

00:07:36.863 --> 00:07:40.969
in part in response to the needs
associated with bringing soldiers

00:07:40.969 --> 00:07:42.920
back from the war.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:45.814
I'd like to think about what the pandemic,
so

00:07:45.814 --> 00:07:51.160
these major events in history shift or
can shift how the society functions.

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:53.025
And so as we think about the pandemic,

00:07:53.025 --> 00:07:57.047
what have we learned about what we need in
terms of education and training, what is

00:07:57.047 --> 00:08:00.926
it revealed maybe that was there that
we weren't paying enough attention to.

00:08:00.926 --> 00:08:03.490
And David out, I'll go to you with that.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.410
Yeah, I think it's been
incredibly revealing.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.841
I think a lot of parents in
particular were shocked first by how

00:08:11.841 --> 00:08:15.899
rigid their school systems were and
how difficult it was for

00:08:15.899 --> 00:08:21.610
them to adjust when the world changed and
suddenly they had to go remote.

00:08:21.610 --> 00:08:26.443
And then by the low quality of the
education their children were getting when

00:08:26.443 --> 00:08:28.620
they were remote.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:33.601
The community college system may
have been built for the 1950s and

00:08:33.601 --> 00:08:38.593
1960s but the K 12 system was built for
the 1910s and 20s.

00:08:38.593 --> 00:08:41.960
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was really built as an industrial

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.350
economy emerged.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:50.590
And it was built on the modern model at
the time, which was called bureaucracy.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.780
These top down hierarchical,
large centralized bureaucracies.

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:59.410
And in the public sector,
they had monopoly status.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.730
They were built to be very stable.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:07.938
To give you the same thing this year you
got last year and the year before and

00:09:07.938 --> 00:09:11.972
the year before that, and
they're very hard to change.

00:09:11.972 --> 00:09:16.346
In the 50s and 60s and 70s,
they unionized in most of America and

00:09:16.346 --> 00:09:18.660
that made them even more rigid.

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:22.960
And so now I think people have realized,
my goodness,

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:30.250
we need schools that can change and that
can use technology and we don't have them.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:34.570
We have a system that is
almost incapable of change.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:39.609
On top of that people realized, our
kids are all different and some of them

00:09:39.609 --> 00:09:44.750
thrive in remote online education and
some of them It doesn't work at all.

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:49.953
Some of them need the social
interaction and so

00:09:49.953 --> 00:09:56.487
all kinds of families began trying to
create pods and micro schools, crew up and

00:09:56.487 --> 00:10:02.620
all kinds of alternatives because
we need choices for different kids.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.062
And when you have a monolithic system that
kind of treats all the kids the same and

00:10:07.062 --> 00:10:10.575
batch processes as they might,
they're on an assembly line,

00:10:10.575 --> 00:10:14.370
people are finally waking up
to realize that doesn't work.

00:10:14.370 --> 00:10:18.500
So I hope it's some kind of turning point.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:21.468
&gt;&gt; And, if I could add on
that in the Higher Ed Space,

00:10:21.468 --> 00:10:24.088
we saw the most fascinating
segmentation of

00:10:24.088 --> 00:10:28.155
two things that people had bundled
into one for a long time, right?

00:10:28.155 --> 00:10:29.545
What is college?

00:10:29.545 --> 00:10:32.971
And what we saw is that people who are
hiring I like to think of the student as

00:10:32.971 --> 00:10:34.500
a consumer.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:39.626
People who are hiring college for a coming
of age experience were devastated right?

00:10:39.626 --> 00:10:44.311
There was no social emotional learning,
the dorm experience learning how to

00:10:44.311 --> 00:10:48.203
become an adult, all those things
that are really intrinsic for

00:10:48.203 --> 00:10:52.111
the 27% of American learners who
ever spend a night in a dorm.

00:10:52.111 --> 00:10:54.850
For the other 73% it was so empowering.

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:59.433
Suddenly they didn't have to take the bus
to get to a local campus in order to sit

00:10:59.433 --> 00:11:03.598
in front of faculty member who spoke for
an hour and a half and then try and

00:11:03.598 --> 00:11:07.560
get their way home or
organize that around their work schedule.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:11.385
Suddenly there was this blossoming of
what they were hiring college for,

00:11:11.385 --> 00:11:13.510
which was skills acquisition.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:17.290
And so I think that's been something
that's been really fascinating as in

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.530
the same way that K 12 is getting
to have a conversation about,

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.170
well, what does the student
need from this system?

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:27.043
We're finding that folks over
the age of 22 are saying, wow,

00:11:27.043 --> 00:11:31.000
college suddenly started being
more what I wanted college to be.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:37.784
&gt;&gt; So the barriers that you pointed out I
think in my understanding is that Guild is

00:11:37.784 --> 00:11:41.372
in part an attempt to get beyond
some of those barriers for

00:11:41.372 --> 00:11:43.190
working age people at ease.

00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:47.488
Can you tell us a little bit about
your thinking about that in Guild?

00:11:47.488 --> 00:11:48.858
How it works in Guild?

00:11:48.858 --> 00:11:52.978
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so our focus demographic or
the 100 million Amercians for

00:11:52.978 --> 00:11:57.600
whom we believe in the labor data tells
us they're going to need to rescale or

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.571
upskill in the next 10 years.

00:11:59.571 --> 00:12:03.920
In order to remain employable and ideally
if we do our jobs have a really good

00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:07.660
chance at a stable family
sustaining wage and career.

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:12.722
And so the way we go about that is
aiming to work with about 300 of

00:12:12.722 --> 00:12:18.154
the 7,500 schools in the US that
we've found have high quality,

00:12:18.154 --> 00:12:21.670
low cost outcomes for working adults.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:25.987
I wish that number was bigger but
300 is enough and they're growing.

00:12:25.987 --> 00:12:31.282
So we work with those schools to help any
employee at companies that we partner

00:12:31.282 --> 00:12:36.414
with like AAA or Discover Financial or
Lowe's or Disney, you name it, but

00:12:36.414 --> 00:12:42.550
mostly frontline workers across the US to
figure out what are their skills today.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:47.021
What do they want out of their
career path in the future?

00:12:47.021 --> 00:12:48.660
A conversation many have never
had the privilege of having.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.950
Then what skills do they then need
to acquire to get on that path?

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:57.411
And 50% of that time, the answer's
getting into a degree program.

00:12:57.411 --> 00:13:02.543
But 50% of the time it's things like
certificates in digital marketing for

00:13:02.543 --> 00:13:07.285
people who've been in analog marketing
frontline jobs for a long time or

00:13:07.285 --> 00:13:11.639
helping people move from being MA
data entry Associate to actually

00:13:11.639 --> 00:13:16.562
becoming a data analyst or actually
the biggest one is people management.

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:18.776
The fastest path to the middle class
right now is teaching someone how

00:13:18.776 --> 00:13:19.510
to lead other people.

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:22.572
So a lot of short term kind
of 6 to 12 month programs and

00:13:22.572 --> 00:13:25.977
then we just give them a coach and
advisor all the way through.

00:13:25.977 --> 00:13:30.408
Which is the recordings that we had coming
out of the community college system was

00:13:30.408 --> 00:13:34.280
that the best thing you can do
is pair someone with a coach.

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.293
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:13:35.293 --> 00:13:40.414
And so, there seems to be working with
the businesses to make this happen and

00:13:40.414 --> 00:13:45.296
one of the things that I'm really
interested in is the respective role of

00:13:45.296 --> 00:13:50.115
business and government in providing
things like education, right?

00:13:50.115 --> 00:13:51.190
So a big social need.

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:56.045
Should business be such
a big player in this space?

00:13:56.045 --> 00:13:58.260
&gt;&gt; I can argue both ways.

00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.930
So I'll tell you my thoughts.

00:13:59.930 --> 00:14:03.560
The fun thing is that Guild was my
graduate school research project.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:06.009
So I've looked at it from every angle, and

00:14:06.009 --> 00:14:09.479
I've sort of maybe annoyed my
investors from time to time for

00:14:09.479 --> 00:14:13.925
doing this, but I can criticize it,
[LAUGH] As well as I couldn't support it.

00:14:13.925 --> 00:14:17.664
I think that on one angle,
the body was the unit of production for

00:14:17.664 --> 00:14:19.000
a really long time.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:23.274
And that's what got us focused on making
sure that employers were caring for

00:14:23.274 --> 00:14:25.690
people's physicality and their bodies.

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.760
Now I think that went too far when we said
should employers be the only place where

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:31.479
people get health care, right?

00:14:31.479 --> 00:14:34.167
But I do believe employers
should care about their

00:14:34.167 --> 00:14:37.960
workers physicality when that
was the unit of production.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:40.760
Today, the unit of production
is the brain, right?

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:42.320
We're in a knowledge economy.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.223
And so I think it makes a ton of sense for

00:14:44.223 --> 00:14:48.165
employers to have a voice in saying here's
what the future of work looks like.

00:14:48.165 --> 00:14:52.366
And we have both data self sourced from
university presidents who say they trust

00:14:52.366 --> 00:14:56.504
employers more now to say what the future
of work looks like then they can figure

00:14:56.504 --> 00:14:58.155
out at their own school.

00:14:58.155 --> 00:15:02.939
But more importantly, average American
now would trust their CEO more than their

00:15:02.939 --> 00:15:07.595
local college president to tell them
what skills they need 10 years from now.

00:15:07.595 --> 00:15:09.350
So that's the role.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.422
That's the role of informing and

00:15:11.422 --> 00:15:15.493
giving influence on the learning
which I'm very supportive of.

00:15:15.493 --> 00:15:20.510
When it comes to funding, we're
vehemently anti student debt at Guild.

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:24.618
And so the path to avoiding that
has been having employers pay.

00:15:24.618 --> 00:15:28.820
I like a world where employers and
the federal government pay.

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:31.330
And I don't think we're paying enough
out of the federal government.

00:15:31.330 --> 00:15:36.200
So I think that's where if the world
changes Guild will be very happy to adapt.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:40.471
&gt;&gt; Great, I don't know Dave if
you have any thoughts about this?

00:15:40.471 --> 00:15:43.920
I mean, I know that you think a lot of
schools, I'd love to hear your thoughts.

00:15:43.920 --> 00:15:48.707
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the reality is
that business does have a huge

00:15:48.707 --> 00:15:51.748
role in education broadly defined.

00:15:51.748 --> 00:15:58.060
If you think about the training and
education that people get post college or

00:15:58.060 --> 00:16:04.620
post high school if they don't go to
college, who provides most of it?

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:08.074
Businesses to their employees.

00:16:08.074 --> 00:16:13.179
And people in government tend to
think about well, we should have this

00:16:13.179 --> 00:16:17.091
program and we should have that program,
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:16:17.091 --> 00:16:20.108
&gt;&gt; There are something like 50 different

00:16:20.108 --> 00:16:25.074
programs funded by the federal government,
most of them small but

00:16:25.074 --> 00:16:31.297
what they really should be thinking about
is, most of the money is fed by business.

00:16:31.297 --> 00:16:35.926
And our role should be to give
people the information they need

00:16:35.926 --> 00:16:39.219
about the quality of
different providers so

00:16:39.219 --> 00:16:44.930
that they can make better decisions
about buying, training or education.

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:49.756
And then as Rachel said,
providing funds for individuals,

00:16:49.756 --> 00:16:53.825
workers and
those who want to be in the workforce, so

00:16:53.825 --> 00:16:58.178
that they can access quality training and
education,

00:16:58.178 --> 00:17:03.499
because low income people don't
have access to those resources.

00:17:03.499 --> 00:17:08.828
So we have to understand that, there's
a market for education and training,

00:17:08.828 --> 00:17:14.013
and most of the providers are businesses,
and that's the world we live in.

00:17:14.013 --> 00:17:17.395
So let's figure out how to make
it work better for people.

00:17:17.395 --> 00:17:21.007
&gt;&gt; I'm really interested in how we
make it work better because Rachel,

00:17:21.007 --> 00:17:23.998
you pointed out that healthcare
is tied to employment and

00:17:23.998 --> 00:17:28.192
part ways you described it because the
body was the unit of production, right?

00:17:28.192 --> 00:17:32.205
And what we really want to go to a world
where we have the same kind of thing for

00:17:32.205 --> 00:17:34.499
knowledge, like, I don't know, like,

00:17:34.499 --> 00:17:38.739
I'm not sure that that sounds like a
better world than the world we're in now.

00:17:38.739 --> 00:17:42.971
So how do you avoid getting
to a place where education is

00:17:42.971 --> 00:17:47.019
incredibly tied up in private
industry as opposed to

00:17:47.019 --> 00:17:51.631
something that society takes
care of across the lifespan?

00:17:51.631 --> 00:17:55.471
&gt;&gt; So I think about it the way
our students talk about it, so

00:17:55.471 --> 00:17:59.148
I got to walk into Stanford
with a bunch of AP credits,

00:17:59.148 --> 00:18:04.337
because I went to a high school system and
I followed the traditional path.

00:18:04.337 --> 00:18:08.940
But our average student is in their 30s,
often a woman, often a mom,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:10.704
often a woman of color, and

00:18:10.704 --> 00:18:15.104
she has often received enormous
amount of training at her company.

00:18:15.104 --> 00:18:19.829
And yet our higher Ed system kind of puts
its nose up to that learning that she's

00:18:19.829 --> 00:18:24.624
done over often, 10 to 14 years,
whereas I've got high school teachers,

00:18:24.624 --> 00:18:25.985
but let's be honest,

00:18:25.985 --> 00:18:30.950
did I really have 12 credits of college
learning the day I walked into Stanford?

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:35.682
I don't think so, and but we honor it,
right, so we create systems that honor one

00:18:35.682 --> 00:18:40.555
population and it's typically populations
that follow the traditional norm which

00:18:40.555 --> 00:18:43.798
then doubles down on privilege and
eliminates access.

00:18:43.798 --> 00:18:47.707
So I think what higher Ed needs to do
is open up the doors to honoring more

00:18:47.707 --> 00:18:48.386
learning.

00:18:48.386 --> 00:18:52.923
The uncomfortable truth in that is that
it's problematic for higher Ed's revenue

00:18:52.923 --> 00:18:57.263
model because higher Ed today has very
high cost of acquisition very, very Very

00:18:57.263 --> 00:19:01.732
high the top 100 don't put the other
7400 schools in the US pay a tremendous

00:19:01.732 --> 00:19:06.179
amount to Google and Facebook and digital
marketing to attract their students.

00:19:06.179 --> 00:19:09.365
And then they have to receive enough
revenue on the back end to make that

00:19:09.365 --> 00:19:10.391
investment worth it.

00:19:10.391 --> 00:19:15.242
So short term programs are very
threatening to universities, but also so

00:19:15.242 --> 00:19:19.936
is giving away credits for free now,
again, elite schools can do it but

00:19:19.936 --> 00:19:24.418
we've not Said we would honor
the learning that happens on the job.

00:19:24.418 --> 00:19:31.300
And I think I think business wants that,
and I think higher Ed is resisting that.

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:35.880
&gt;&gt; Great, so I also want to shift
it a little bit from higher Ed and

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.095
think about primary school.

00:19:38.095 --> 00:19:42.851
So as I assume many of you listening
know and I'm sure Rachel and

00:19:42.851 --> 00:19:46.622
David you know that there
are huge disparities and

00:19:46.622 --> 00:19:50.754
outcomes along certainly
among dimensions of race but

00:19:50.754 --> 00:19:56.089
class as well in terms of educational
achievement, reading, Math.

00:19:56.089 --> 00:20:01.553
And then we also see across all different
demographic groups, as someone pointed

00:20:01.553 --> 00:20:07.194
out, a stunning and dismaying consistency
in misinformation in civic education.

00:20:07.194 --> 00:20:13.597
Our citizenry just does not understand
how our government functions.

00:20:13.597 --> 00:20:14.863
Why is this?

00:20:14.863 --> 00:20:18.793
Like what do you think
is causing the failure?

00:20:18.793 --> 00:20:23.897
And now David, I'll go to you and I know
you've already talked about how it's

00:20:23.897 --> 00:20:28.999
an old system, but the old system was
designed to teach people to read and write

00:20:28.999 --> 00:20:34.054
and math and we're failing at that too,
so what do you think is going on here?

00:20:34.054 --> 00:20:37.996
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think that when we created the system,

00:20:37.996 --> 00:20:43.319
which as I said is very stable,
and schools today are not all that

00:20:43.319 --> 00:20:48.855
different from they were when I
was going to school 50 years ago.

00:20:48.855 --> 00:20:54.366
They assume that students
come to school motivated and

00:20:54.366 --> 00:20:59.528
then the teachers will
provide knowledge to them.

00:20:59.528 --> 00:21:04.648
And that still works in a lot of middle
and upper middle class communities.

00:21:04.648 --> 00:21:09.900
Because many of those students
do come to school motivated and

00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:14.748
they managed to get an education,
but In the inner city,

00:21:14.748 --> 00:21:19.192
and in some rural areas with
really low income kids,

00:21:19.192 --> 00:21:26.481
you can't make that assumption they might
not even come to school speaking English.

00:21:26.481 --> 00:21:30.883
They may grow up in a neighborhood where
they don't know anyone except their

00:21:30.883 --> 00:21:33.015
teachers who ever went to college and

00:21:33.015 --> 00:21:36.761
it's never occurred to them that
they would ever go to college.

00:21:36.761 --> 00:21:42.169
And so, what happens is they do okay
through elementary school typically but

00:21:42.169 --> 00:21:47.076
then you hit middle school and
you start introducing extra actions and

00:21:47.076 --> 00:21:50.089
asking them to learn things like algebra.

00:21:50.089 --> 00:21:54.785
And why would you ever learn
algebra unless you saw it as a step

00:21:54.785 --> 00:21:58.020
toward college or a step toward a career?

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:03.377
And if they don't see that
the motivation isn't there and

00:22:03.377 --> 00:22:09.947
they fall off the conveyor belt and
you see high dropout rates and so on.

00:22:09.947 --> 00:22:15.060
We need schools that meet
those kids where they are,

00:22:15.060 --> 00:22:18.903
the first job is to create motivation.

00:22:18.903 --> 00:22:20.409
It's interesting,

00:22:20.409 --> 00:22:26.192
we've created a innovation sector in
public education called charter schools.

00:22:26.192 --> 00:22:30.020
And they work, well in some places and
not as well and

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:34.110
others a lot depends on
the state law that governs them.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:40.276
And a lot depends on whether they're
authorized by a school district or

00:22:40.276 --> 00:22:45.206
a state board or a university,
but where they work well,

00:22:45.206 --> 00:22:50.345
when you visit one of these schools,
they'll immediately

00:22:50.345 --> 00:22:54.790
tell you yeah, on day one,
our job is motivation.

00:22:54.790 --> 00:22:58.053
We bring the kids in let's say
we're a high school we bring

00:22:58.053 --> 00:23:01.326
them in in ninth grade for
two weeks before school starts.

00:23:01.326 --> 00:23:06.314
And it's all focused on building
motivation, building expectations.

00:23:06.314 --> 00:23:11.517
Then we tailor to where they are,
we don't just give them the classic ninth

00:23:11.517 --> 00:23:17.401
grade curriculum and if they're ready for
it great and then if they're not too bad.

00:23:17.401 --> 00:23:21.661
We meet them where they are, we provide
tutors as they need to catch up,

00:23:21.661 --> 00:23:26.283
we provide extra time in the summer and
during breaks if they need to catch up.

00:23:26.283 --> 00:23:30.092
The word the cliché has
become personalization, but

00:23:30.092 --> 00:23:35.506
we really do need to go from batch
processing to personalizing education and

00:23:35.506 --> 00:23:40.326
helping each student go from where
they are when they get to school to

00:23:40.326 --> 00:23:44.237
where they need to be, and
the old system can't do it.

00:23:44.237 --> 00:23:49.649
It's just impossible, it's so
rule driven and so rigid and

00:23:49.649 --> 00:23:54.222
so built really for
the convenience of the adults.

00:23:54.222 --> 00:23:59.039
Not for the needs of the children that
we need fundamental change in how that

00:23:59.039 --> 00:24:00.270
system operates.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:05.811
And Rachel, I know this is not your space,
but I'm just curious if you have thoughts.

00:24:05.811 --> 00:24:09.201
I know you spent a lot of time
thinking about education.

00:24:10.220 --> 00:24:14.945
What do you see as a possibility
in terms of improving outcomes?

00:24:14.945 --> 00:24:17.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can't profess to be an expert.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.869
I kind of sit on the barbells because
I spend my time in higher Ed and

00:24:20.869 --> 00:24:22.870
then we run a preschool daycare at Guild.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:27.163
So I spend a lot of time thinking about
innovation in childcare, which by the way,

00:24:27.163 --> 00:24:29.570
that's another mess for another day.

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:32.950
It's worse than K-12 on a bad day.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:37.081
But in the K-12 space, I think one
of the things we've lost our way on,

00:24:37.081 --> 00:24:41.883
especially amidst the movement to send
everybody to college, which I'm culturally

00:24:41.883 --> 00:24:45.638
very supportive of, I think we
have to define what college means.

00:24:45.638 --> 00:24:50.258
But I'm culturally supportive of where
that came from and the moving away from

00:24:50.258 --> 00:24:55.180
a culture that tracked people based on
race and socio-economic background.

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:59.598
But when we shifted to this world, where
about 10% of Americans went to college to

00:24:59.598 --> 00:25:03.110
where that was supposed to be
the aspiration for everyone.

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:07.275
What we forgot to say was, so
how do we want to measure that and

00:25:07.275 --> 00:25:12.030
we got really obsessed with basically
the memorization of content.

00:25:12.030 --> 00:25:16.956
So much of our K-12 system is around
trying to get you to memorize and

00:25:16.956 --> 00:25:18.720
accumulate knowledge.

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:25.291
But we've computers and I'm pretty sure my
kids are going to use a search engine and

00:25:25.291 --> 00:25:30.330
a calculator 3x more than I did and
30x more than their grandparents did.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:31.686
So I'm pretty sure for my kids,

00:25:31.686 --> 00:25:34.900
the measurement is going to be do they
love learning and do they love it enough

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:38.040
to constantly contextualize
the knowledge they're learning.

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.562
And I don't think any of those tests that
can measure whether they've memorized

00:25:41.562 --> 00:25:44.000
anything are really going to matter.

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:47.949
I'd probably fail my seventh grade algebra
quiz if I retook it right now, but

00:25:47.949 --> 00:25:51.840
I was able to build on top of that
learning to do the things I do today.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:53.750
And we kind of have to reset.

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:56.974
I just think we have to reset
the conversation because to do the social

00:25:56.974 --> 00:26:00.531
emotional learning, to care for people,
to do what actually I think some of

00:26:00.531 --> 00:26:03.830
the system did better believe it or
not in the 20s ,30s and 40s.

00:26:03.830 --> 00:26:06.820
Not for everyone by a long stretch.

00:26:06.820 --> 00:26:09.124
But what the system did well, for

00:26:09.124 --> 00:26:14.330
at least the middle class white male
was not about memorizing content.

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:18.037
It was about providing a platform
to become the type of adult society

00:26:18.037 --> 00:26:19.280
wanted you to become.

00:26:21.510 --> 00:26:24.957
And so these are all great aspirations but
as as you both know,

00:26:24.957 --> 00:26:30.160
better than almost anyone, there are a lot
of barriers to making these changes.

00:26:30.160 --> 00:26:34.722
And I'm going to come back to
something you said earlier, David,

00:26:34.722 --> 00:26:39.070
that unions you mentioned
as one barrier to change.

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:43.406
And that's actually is a big part
of the conversation we talked about

00:26:43.406 --> 00:26:47.222
education teachers unions
are particularly powerful, but

00:26:47.222 --> 00:26:50.230
they also are way presumably
to protect labor.

00:26:50.230 --> 00:26:54.630
So our teachers aren't paid very well and
last I looked at a 2013 study,

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:59.104
that suggested that Charter schools
teachers are paid less than traditional

00:26:59.104 --> 00:27:01.070
public school teachers.

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:04.580
So as you think about,
making these kind of changes.

00:27:04.580 --> 00:27:09.508
What do you tell the workforce,
the current teachers, primary,

00:27:09.508 --> 00:27:14.530
secondary school, about what they
can expect from these changes?

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:22.950
&gt;&gt; Well, I think that in many ways
the teachers are the most disillusioned

00:27:22.950 --> 00:27:28.510
part of this whole picture,
teachers in public school systems.

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:34.390
Gallup does a survey,
has done it millions of times, for

00:27:34.390 --> 00:27:37.450
all kinds of businesses and professions.

00:27:38.810 --> 00:27:43.350
I think they there are about a dozen
professions they've surveyed

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:46.290
&gt;&gt; And one of the questions it's,

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:49.560
I forget it's 20 questions or
16 questions.

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:57.920
One of the questions is, I feel that my
opinions matter at work most of the time.

00:27:59.400 --> 00:28:04.550
And of all those professions education
is the highest and saying no to that.

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:11.260
Public school teachers feel like that
they're at the bottom of the barrel and

00:28:11.260 --> 00:28:15.673
outside their school classroom,
they have little authority,

00:28:15.673 --> 00:28:19.940
they have no control over sort
of the direction of the school.

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:26.320
And their complaints sort of boil
down to they treat us like children.

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:34.096
We need to make education a professional
and in a profession you have

00:28:34.096 --> 00:28:38.842
some role in helping to manage,
helping to run the institution.

00:28:41.969 --> 00:28:48.545
The teachers unionized for good reasons
back in the 60s and 70s, it was needed.

00:28:48.545 --> 00:28:51.215
Women in particular were
just treated terribly.

00:28:51.215 --> 00:28:56.149
Back in the 50s, if you got
pregnant as a teacher you were out.

00:28:56.149 --> 00:29:02.490
So, we needed all of those changes.

00:29:02.490 --> 00:29:07.562
But now we need to move away from
a model that's built on an industrial

00:29:07.562 --> 00:29:12.722
workforce management versus labor
to a more professional model where

00:29:12.722 --> 00:29:18.600
the teachers are part of the team that
runs the school and that's a tough shift.

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:21.149
It's happening,

00:29:21.149 --> 00:29:25.532
there are all about 150 teacher run
public schools in this country,

00:29:25.532 --> 00:29:30.660
about half of them charter, half of them
district, and they're growing slowly.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:37.958
But all of the status quo, all of
the forces that preserve the status quo

00:29:37.958 --> 00:29:44.360
in K-12 education stand in the way of
that and one of them is teachers unions.

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.950
They actually don't want teachers
treated like professionals.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:55.566
They don't want teachers to be able to

00:29:55.566 --> 00:29:59.930
move from school to school,
as they choose.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:06.861
They want districts preserved as they are,
to preserve the protections

00:30:06.861 --> 00:30:12.034
that teachers have, for example,
the fact that after the first two or

00:30:12.034 --> 00:30:16.169
three years that it's almost
impossible to fire them.

00:30:16.169 --> 00:30:19.989
And that's very important
to the teachers unions.

00:30:19.989 --> 00:30:25.941
And that creates so
much rigidity in the system and

00:30:25.941 --> 00:30:33.749
undermines the ability to make
schools improve year after year.

00:30:33.749 --> 00:30:37.118
&gt;&gt; My mom was a school
teacher by the way and

00:30:37.118 --> 00:30:43.365
she was quite frustrated because most
primary school teachers are in Cunby.

00:30:43.365 --> 00:30:48.325
But I think she also appreciates
the protections, right?

00:30:48.325 --> 00:30:52.665
So I think there's a lot of distrust
in the people who are making decisions

00:30:52.665 --> 00:30:55.440
about how resources are expended.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:58.790
And the union is a way to have voice.

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:00.630
It's obviously collective voice.

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:06.303
I wonder how do you change the system such
that you maintain, I hear you saying that

00:31:06.303 --> 00:31:10.990
they should be treated like professionals,
they should have a role in running things.

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:14.830
That is not at all I would argue
how it currently functions.

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:18.968
And so if you get rid of unions
prior to making that change,

00:31:18.968 --> 00:31:21.750
then you put teachers at risk.

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:24.284
So I just wonder how do you think
about that chicken and egg problem?

00:31:28.852 --> 00:31:32.600
&gt;&gt; We need to preserve
certain protections.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:41.914
So for example,
if you're in a school district,

00:31:41.914 --> 00:31:45.809
you should be protected from

00:31:45.809 --> 00:31:50.690
the whim of administrator firing.

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:54.460
There should be due process,
there should be a process.

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:59.506
When I was a consultant, our firm
actually was hired to run the Minneapolis

00:31:59.506 --> 00:32:04.110
school system to three and
a half years, back in the 1990s.

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:06.510
They actually wanted
to hire our president.

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:10.810
But he wouldn't leave the company he just
founded and so they hired the company.

00:32:11.860 --> 00:32:16.828
And one thing that he and his colleagues
managed to do was work with the union in

00:32:16.828 --> 00:32:20.727
Minneapolis to develop
a teacher-run system to weeding out

00:32:20.727 --> 00:32:23.720
teachers who shouldn't
be in the classroom.

00:32:25.030 --> 00:32:25.780
We can do that.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:31.913
It means modifying the tenure system,
not eliminating it completely.

00:32:35.189 --> 00:32:40.760
The same with if we change our
school system so that principals and

00:32:40.760 --> 00:32:46.334
their teachers have more power to
actually operate the school and

00:32:46.334 --> 00:32:50.893
change the learning model to
meet the needs of the kids

00:32:50.893 --> 00:32:55.680
who showed up at that school,
then teachers need more.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:00.798
Some teachers won't fit this school, or
let's say this school decides to become

00:33:00.798 --> 00:33:04.549
a dual language immersion school because
there's a lot of Spanish speaking kids.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:08.260
And it's far more effective
in that environment.

00:33:10.030 --> 00:33:15.230
Well, some teachers won't speak Spanish
and they'll lose their job at that school.

00:33:15.230 --> 00:33:18.820
But a district can say you're
not going to lose your job.

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:23.532
We're going to let you choose a place at
a school that will fit you better with

00:33:23.532 --> 00:33:29.010
the principal who wants you but
is not a dual language immersion school.

00:33:29.010 --> 00:33:34.753
So there are ways we can create more
flexible systems while continuing

00:33:34.753 --> 00:33:39.913
some legitimate and very fair and
common sense protections for

00:33:39.913 --> 00:33:43.927
teachers and frankly,
teachers welcome that.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:52.445
They are very frustrated if
you read any of the surveys.

00:33:52.445 --> 00:33:56.230
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think there's not
a lot of disagreement with that.

00:33:56.230 --> 00:33:59.530
There's this frustrating being
a teacher these days for sure.

00:33:59.530 --> 00:34:02.752
I want to kind of shift back to
thinking on the student side and

00:34:02.752 --> 00:34:04.340
later stage in life.

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:07.210
We don't have a lot of,
in this country, vocational training.

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:12.120
And so this is obviously going to be for
you, Rachel.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:18.237
And by vocational training, I don't
mean what people think of is plumbers or

00:34:18.237 --> 00:34:21.020
electricians though,
the kind of basic trades.

00:34:21.020 --> 00:34:25.130
But I mean vocational training that
could be even like tech and engineering.

00:34:25.130 --> 00:34:28.504
We have a university system and
then people go out,

00:34:28.504 --> 00:34:32.590
and often really learn what they
need to do at the job, right?

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:34.810
That is a common, I think, experience.

00:34:36.220 --> 00:34:41.300
How do we address the historic inequities
in that kind of training, for you, Rachel?

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.663
So I think now people will say,
it's a pipeline issue, but

00:34:44.663 --> 00:34:48.839
the reality is when the great jobs were
factory jobs, there was still racial and

00:34:48.839 --> 00:34:50.860
gender inequities then too, right?

00:34:50.860 --> 00:34:55.000
So it's not as if these inequities are new
because we've now moved to this knowledge

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:55.533
economy.

00:34:55.533 --> 00:35:00.087
The inequities that we have now are the
same inequities we had before in terms of

00:35:00.087 --> 00:35:02.745
access to the best paying jobs.

00:35:02.745 --> 00:35:07.932
And so as you think about what you're
doing the way you're trying to upskill and

00:35:07.932 --> 00:35:10.565
prepare people for these array of jobs,

00:35:10.565 --> 00:35:15.405
how do you address that historic
inequity that continues?

00:35:15.405 --> 00:35:20.364
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so at one level, because
this is a philosophical environment,

00:35:20.364 --> 00:35:23.540
I feel like I can go dork on history here.

00:35:23.540 --> 00:35:27.351
Part of why America has fewer,
I'll call it technical and vocational,

00:35:27.351 --> 00:35:31.476
right, because we called it technical
schools until we started using tech for

00:35:31.476 --> 00:35:35.400
another word,
is we've a really heterogeneous economy.

00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:37.049
And that's actually great, right?

00:35:37.049 --> 00:35:41.442
So when you look at the countries that
have really successful apprenticeship and

00:35:41.442 --> 00:35:46.353
technical trainings, whether that's moving
people into what's called the historical

00:35:46.353 --> 00:35:50.229
trades, which I'm really passionate about,
or moving people into,

00:35:50.229 --> 00:35:53.159
let's call it the 21st
century technical trades.

00:35:53.159 --> 00:35:57.090
Companies doing that well have
really homogeneous economies.

00:35:57.090 --> 00:36:00.042
So the government and
business tend to align, I'm like, okay,

00:36:00.042 --> 00:36:01.229
what are our five things?

00:36:01.229 --> 00:36:04.511
There are countries in Europe who are
like, we're a car manufacturing country.

00:36:04.511 --> 00:36:08.219
[LAUGH] And others who are like,
we make cheese and chocolate, right?

00:36:08.219 --> 00:36:09.370
[LAUGH] That's actually a thing.

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:12.280
America, good,
it's part of why our GDP grows.

00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:13.477
We're heterogeneous.

00:36:13.477 --> 00:36:18.478
So if you're going to then try and
do something that creates the opportunity,

00:36:18.478 --> 00:36:23.551
what we used to do is we did it regionally
and the US had a very regional economy for

00:36:23.551 --> 00:36:28.441
horrible reasons, mostly connected
into slavery and then the way we grew.

00:36:28.441 --> 00:36:31.895
But now we have a national
heterogeneous economy.

00:36:31.895 --> 00:36:34.457
So you have to start there when
you're talking to anyone who lives

00:36:34.457 --> 00:36:35.255
anywhere in the US.

00:36:35.255 --> 00:36:38.990
They don't want to feel constrained to
what their pathways can be based on their

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:42.802
the region they grew up in or,
to your question, their background.

00:36:42.802 --> 00:36:45.556
So then the next question we
often talk a lot about is, okay,

00:36:45.556 --> 00:36:47.610
how do we actually do
this in the right order?

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:50.941
And my main thing that I think we need
to do is that we need to actually flip

00:36:50.941 --> 00:36:52.562
the order of learning.

00:36:52.562 --> 00:36:56.644
Today we say, go get your general studies,
go learn about critical thinking and

00:36:56.644 --> 00:36:57.562
all these things.

00:36:57.562 --> 00:37:01.138
And then we'll teach you some technical
skills maybe in your senior year, or

00:37:01.138 --> 00:37:04.060
better yet [LAUGH] I'll pick
on Stanford for a second here.

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:07.524
We don't have a business degree,
if you want that, you come back for

00:37:07.524 --> 00:37:10.110
graduate school, [LAUGH] and
hook line and sinker.

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:13.200
I did it, but that's a problem.

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:16.508
I think we should teach people
the technical skills first,

00:37:16.508 --> 00:37:18.832
that allows them to enter the workforce.

00:37:18.832 --> 00:37:22.362
And we need to recognize that we're now
in a lifelong learning economy, and

00:37:22.362 --> 00:37:23.860
how do we stack the learning on?

00:37:23.860 --> 00:37:28.794
And if you change that conversation, you
suddenly open the door to acquiring those

00:37:28.794 --> 00:37:32.190
technical skills to people in
a lot more equitable ways.

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:35.790
And so what I say a lot is, why do you
have to go to college to get a job?

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:41.220
Why can't you get a job, learn and then
decide what you want to do in college?

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.663
&gt;&gt; All right,
that is a very different system.

00:37:46.663 --> 00:37:48.030
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There you go.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:51.856
[LAUGH] You nodded when I said,
can I go philosophical work-

00:37:51.856 --> 00:37:53.875
&gt;&gt; Yeah, of course, no, I love that.

00:37:53.875 --> 00:37:55.470
[LAUGH] Go ahead, David.

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:58.280
&gt;&gt; Our government funding
has to shift as well.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:37:59.168
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:37:59.168 --> 00:38:01.510
&gt;&gt; We're stuck in this,
exactly what Rachel said,

00:38:01.510 --> 00:38:03.250
this mindset that you go to college.

00:38:03.250 --> 00:38:09.032
Well, so, even our most progressive
leaders like Bernie Sanders and

00:38:09.032 --> 00:38:13.350
Elizabeth Warren,
they're all for free college.

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:17.220
But what percent of
Americans go to college?

00:38:18.230 --> 00:38:21.830
What percent of Americans stay
in college for four years?

00:38:21.830 --> 00:38:23.270
It's a minority.

00:38:23.270 --> 00:38:25.330
Most Americans don't.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:26.510
So you're leaving them out.

00:38:26.510 --> 00:38:31.278
You're going to spend all this money
on people who are already a little

00:38:31.278 --> 00:38:32.540
bit privileged.

00:38:34.020 --> 00:38:40.930
I have proposed, not that anyone's
listening, but we should take that money.

00:38:41.990 --> 00:38:45.515
And why don't we say anybody who's making,

00:38:45.515 --> 00:38:49.875
has an income today of
less than $80,000 a year,

00:38:49.875 --> 00:38:54.632
let's give them an account,
a lifelong learning account.

00:38:54.632 --> 00:38:58.360
Let's start with $10,000.

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:02.929
And maybe we'll have a sliding scale and
maybe it'll be at 80,000,

00:39:02.929 --> 00:39:08.161
it'll be 5,000 and down when you
make 70,000, it'll be 10,000.

00:39:08.161 --> 00:39:10.850
So, it phases out slowly but the point is,

00:39:10.850 --> 00:39:14.610
let's give everybody
resources that they can use.

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:17.594
And if they want to use that to
go to community college, great.

00:39:17.594 --> 00:39:20.639
If they want to use that to help pay for
a four-year college, great.

00:39:20.639 --> 00:39:24.796
But if they're already
in the workforce and

00:39:24.796 --> 00:39:30.297
they know they need certain skills,
fine, go at night and

00:39:30.297 --> 00:39:35.801
get a certificate or
use the money how you need it to improve

00:39:35.801 --> 00:39:40.886
your skills and
your education at your point of life.

00:39:40.886 --> 00:39:44.830
And let's make it available for
the bottom half of our income scale,

00:39:44.830 --> 00:39:45.850
not the top half.

00:39:47.720 --> 00:39:49.690
&gt;&gt; All right, so
you gotta jump on my last question.

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:53.636
So I'll go with Rachael, see if you have
another, something else you want to add.

00:39:53.636 --> 00:39:57.710
But this whole series is
about reimagining work.

00:39:57.710 --> 00:40:02.479
So, what would you do if you
could reimagine the way education

00:40:02.479 --> 00:40:04.130
functions in work?

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:05.934
What would be your primary focus?

00:40:05.934 --> 00:40:07.992
What would you like to change?

00:40:07.992 --> 00:40:12.950
Yeah, so David hit it but the foreign
40 is dead, right, going to college for

00:40:12.950 --> 00:40:17.020
4 years and then going to work for
40 and then retiring, dead.

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:23.696
I think we're entering what I call like
the every four, which is that we're going

00:40:23.696 --> 00:40:27.765
to work and every four to five years
we're going to need to pick our heads up.

00:40:27.765 --> 00:40:31.130
And either reskill meaning prepare for
our next job or

00:40:31.130 --> 00:40:34.364
upskill get better at
today's job that we're in.

00:40:34.364 --> 00:40:38.329
And what the data tells us is that white
people who start in their career in

00:40:38.329 --> 00:40:41.857
the white collar economy earlier
are going to do more upskilling.

00:40:41.857 --> 00:40:43.006
And unfortunately,

00:40:43.006 --> 00:40:47.340
people who live in the lower half of the
economy are going to need to reskill more,

00:40:47.340 --> 00:40:51.132
which is actually more expensive,
right, learning a whole new job.

00:40:51.132 --> 00:40:55.424
And so I would love to see our
funding infrastructure, as well as our

00:40:55.424 --> 00:41:00.644
conversation around higher ed, and then
most importantly our higher ed entity.

00:41:00.644 --> 00:41:05.852
But in some are already doing this
reorient their business models and

00:41:05.852 --> 00:41:11.883
their ways of teaching and their ways of
working to serve the every for economy.

00:41:11.883 --> 00:41:17.439
&gt;&gt; Great, and David, you have the last
word what would you want to leave us with?

00:41:17.439 --> 00:41:20.577
&gt;&gt; Well, going back to K12 for
a minute, I mean,

00:41:20.577 --> 00:41:23.425
your question about what would you change?

00:41:23.425 --> 00:41:28.339
I think we need a K12 system in
which we have the people who

00:41:28.339 --> 00:41:32.764
run schools actually
are empowered to run schools.

00:41:32.764 --> 00:41:37.450
Rather than some central office making
all the hiring and firing decisions and

00:41:37.450 --> 00:41:38.802
spending decisions.

00:41:38.802 --> 00:41:43.894
And those schools being allowed and
encouraged to diversify their learning

00:41:43.894 --> 00:41:48.826
models, particularly in urban areas
where geography is on your side kids

00:41:48.826 --> 00:41:53.616
families actually have choices in
terms of the schools they can get to.

00:41:53.616 --> 00:41:57.908
So that there are Montessori schools and
there are stem schools and

00:41:57.908 --> 00:42:03.285
there are performing arts schools and
there are dual language immersion schools.

00:42:03.285 --> 00:42:06.815
And on and on and on and
give the families choices so

00:42:06.815 --> 00:42:10.865
that they can find the school
that best fits their child.

00:42:10.865 --> 00:42:15.249
And then hold the schools accountable
if the kids are learning and

00:42:15.249 --> 00:42:18.841
the parents are happy and
the kids are happy, great.

00:42:18.841 --> 00:42:21.149
That school gets to keep going,

00:42:21.149 --> 00:42:25.603
might even be invited to create
another school to replicate.

00:42:25.603 --> 00:42:30.759
But if the parents are gradually
drifting away, taking their kids out and

00:42:30.759 --> 00:42:34.684
the test scores are bad and
the teacher parent surveys.

00:42:34.684 --> 00:42:38.103
And student surveys tell you
that it's an unhappy place,

00:42:38.103 --> 00:42:42.801
that's school should be replaced by a team
that can do better in that building.

00:42:42.801 --> 00:42:46.683
So actual accountability for performance.

00:42:46.683 --> 00:42:51.392
I think that's the kind of 21st
century system that works.

00:42:51.392 --> 00:42:56.478
And we know it works because we have
it in some places like New Orleans,

00:42:56.478 --> 00:43:02.316
which is all charter now, unlike
Washington DC which is half charter now.

00:43:02.316 --> 00:43:06.551
And Denver where Rachel is,
which has a lot of charter schools and

00:43:06.551 --> 00:43:09.788
a lot of innovation schools
within the district.

00:43:09.788 --> 00:43:13.598
Our districts are beginning to
put their toes in that water but

00:43:13.598 --> 00:43:16.171
it's awfully slow and we need more of it.

00:43:16.171 --> 00:43:17.025
&gt;&gt; All right, great thanks.

00:43:17.025 --> 00:43:20.278
Well, I hope we see some of those
changes we are in desperate need

00:43:20.278 --> 00:43:22.763
of some improvements that
you both pointed out.

00:43:22.763 --> 00:43:26.184
So thank you so much for talking with me.

00:43:26.184 --> 00:43:29.736
Next week, we'll discuss the ways
that work controls our lives.

00:43:29.736 --> 00:43:34.132
And we'll be joined by Karen Arnold,
Head of Talent Acquisition at Automatic,

00:43:34.132 --> 00:43:36.602
a company that has always
been fully remote.

00:43:36.602 --> 00:43:40.513
Thank you both again and
that's it for this session.

00:43:40.513 --> 00:43:41.241
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Brian.

00:43:41.241 --> 00:43:42.289
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Brian.

00:43:47.597 --> 00:43:50.728
[MUSIC]

